1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,440
The school Week Four practical links catastrophic bleeding, the CAT
tourniquet, pelvic binding, fractures, spinal protection and chest
injury. Treat these as connected decisions about one changing patient.

2
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:26,016
This lecture follows an original industrial injury, then uses a
separate pelvic example. You will explain not only what device you
choose, but what problem it should change and how you will detect
failure.

3
00:00:26,016 --> 00:00:38,664
Nancy supplies the mechanism and assessment reading. The saved school
outline supplies the practice targets; the provincial examination
snapshot and current service procedures are separately identified.

4
00:00:38,664 --> 00:00:50,232
Reading and narrated rehearsal prepare you for supervised stations.
They do not replace the school practical checkoff, actual device
instruction or the authorization required for patient care.

5
00:00:50,232 --> 00:01:05,520
A forty-one-year-old worker has a thigh trapped by machinery.
Qualified rescuers have isolated the machine. There is continuing
heavy bleeding from the exposed limb and the patient is anxious, cool
and breathing rapidly.

6
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:17,664
The pulse is one hundred twenty-two, pressure one hundred six over
seventy-four, respiratory rate twenty-eight and saturation ninety-
seven percent. The patient can answer questions.

7
00:01:17,664 --> 00:01:29,208
Talking and a preserved systolic pressure do not exclude serious blood
loss. The continuing bleed requires immediate control while the crew
checks the other primary threats.

8
00:01:29,208 --> 00:01:42,024
Do not order an unplanned lift simply because access is difficult.
Coordinate clinical preparation with the rescue lead and request the
resources needed for a safe release and rapid onward care.

9
00:01:42,024 --> 00:01:53,880
Mechanism describes how energy reached the patient. Compression can
damage muscle and vessels locally, while a fall or secondary impact
can create a different injury elsewhere.

10
00:01:53,880 --> 00:02:07,536
Ask what was trapped, how much tissue was involved, how long the
compression lasted and what happened before and after the event.
Obtain rescue information without allowing a long history to postpone
bleeding control.

11
00:02:07,536 --> 00:02:20,568
The visible thigh injury does not clear the chest, abdomen or pelvis.
Make a rapid deliberate search for other significant injuries and
expand the examination as the situation permits.

12
00:02:20,568 --> 00:02:34,344
A mechanism raises or lowers suspicion; clinical findings and their
trend determine the response. Do not diagnose every predicted injury
as present or dismiss a concerning finding after a low-speed event.

13
00:02:34,344 --> 00:02:49,824
MARCH begins with massive hemorrhage, followed by airway,
respirations, circulation and hypothermia or head-injury protection.
In this case, exsanguinating limb bleeding cannot wait for a lengthy
sequential survey.

14
00:02:49,824 --> 00:03:03,408
The current major-trauma guideline calls its sequence C A B C D E,
with catastrophic hemorrhage first. The common purpose is rapid
correction of reversible threats, not a competition between mnemonics.

15
00:03:03,408 --> 00:03:16,848
This does not mean every minor traumatic wound outranks an obstructed
airway. Judge the actual threat; when personnel are available,
hemorrhage control and airway or breathing support proceed in
parallel.

16
00:03:16,848 --> 00:03:28,728
After any intervention or patient change, return to the primary
survey. A mnemonic helps organize attention but cannot prove that
every threat has been found or corrected.

17
00:03:28,728 --> 00:03:39,312
Assign one clinician to the visible bleed and another to airway,
breathing and the wider patient assessment. State tasks aloud and
confirm they are understood.

18
00:03:39,312 --> 00:03:50,592
Agree with the rescue lead how access and release will occur. Rescue
isolation is not a one-time assumption; new movement or equipment can
create another hazard.

19
00:03:50,592 --> 00:04:01,440
Call out whether the bleeding has actually stopped, not merely that
the tourniquet is on. Tell the team immediately about worsening
mentation, breathing or perfusion.

20
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:14,160
Maintain a transfer plan while procedures occur. A difficult vein or
an attractive training task must not make the crew lose awareness of
time, ongoing loss or the need for definitive control.

21
00:04:14,160 --> 00:04:25,464
Saturation describes the proportion of measured hemoglobin binding
sites carrying oxygen. It does not measure the amount of hemoglobin
remaining or the flow reaching the tissues.

22
00:04:25,464 --> 00:04:40,296
With hemorrhage, sympathetic compensation may preserve cuff pressure
while peripheral perfusion deteriorates. A rapid weak pulse, cool
skin, tachypnea or changing behavior may be important before
hypotension.

23
00:04:40,296 --> 00:04:53,736
Use the whole pattern and repeated measurements. Medicines, age, pain,
temperature and spinal injury can alter the expected signs, so no
single absent feature safely excludes shock.

24
00:04:53,736 --> 00:05:06,264
Do not stand or sit an unstable injured patient up to prove
hypovolemia. You already have a clinical problem requiring treatment
and reassessment without a provocative orthostatic test.

25
00:05:06,264 --> 00:05:20,520
A successful dressing or tourniquet controls one source, not the
entire circulation. Major loss can remain concealed in the chest,
abdomen, retroperitoneum, pelvis or injured soft tissues.

26
00:05:20,520 --> 00:05:32,016
Expose and examine purposefully, including the back when it can be
accessed safely. Do not repeatedly roll an unstable patient simply to
finish a routine checklist.

27
00:05:32,016 --> 00:05:45,336
Look for tenderness, deformity, wounds, asymmetric breathing and
evolving perfusion. Early absence of bruising or a normal initial
pressure is not a dependable internal-bleeding rule-out.

28
00:05:45,336 --> 00:05:58,512
If bleeding cannot be definitively controlled in the field, minimize
delay and move toward the appropriate trauma resource. A second bag of
clear fluid cannot substitute for stopping an internal bleed.

29
00:05:58,512 --> 00:06:09,744
Use appropriate protection and expose the relevant area enough to
identify the source. Do not spend time naming the vessel before acting
on an obvious life-threatening bleed.

30
00:06:09,744 --> 00:06:20,304
Apply firm direct pressure at the wound. A dressing is useful only
when it helps deliver effective pressure; loosely covering the blood
is not source control.

31
00:06:20,304 --> 00:06:32,592
Assess whether the bleeding stops. For severe extremity bleeding that
is not controlled promptly, prepare the indicated tourniquet instead
of repeatedly restarting ineffective pressure attempts.

32
00:06:32,592 --> 00:06:46,104
If pressure succeeds, maintain control and secure an appropriate
dressing while continuing the primary survey. Recheck after movement
and do not assume a neat exterior excludes bleeding beneath it.

33
00:06:46,104 --> 00:06:59,040
The tool must fit the anatomy. A limb tourniquet can compress an arm
or leg circumferentially, but cannot be placed around the chest,
abdomen or neck as an ordinary bleeding treatment.

34
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:10,488
Eligible penetrating junctional wounds may benefit from packing when
direct pressure alone is inadequate. The reviewed local procedure
excludes neck, chest and abdominal packing.

35
00:07:10,488 --> 00:07:23,016
Internal bleeding requires a different plan: control what is
accessible, stabilize relevant injuries, preserve temperature and
perfusion, and arrange rapid appropriate definitive care.

36
00:07:23,016 --> 00:07:36,912
A visibly unsuccessful intervention requires reassessment, not merely
repetition of its name. State the failed endpoint and choose the next
indicated action while maintaining any control already achieved.

37
00:07:36,912 --> 00:07:47,544
For the reviewed local limb procedure, place the tourniquet five to
eight centimetres proximal to the injury, or as high on the limb as
needed, and not over a joint.

38
00:07:47,544 --> 00:08:04,008
Current PR03 specifies direct skin contact. Do not introduce generic
padding or place the device over a solid object in clothing; a source-
specific emergency over-clothing instruction is not a universal
deliberate-placement rule.

39
00:08:04,008 --> 00:08:16,416
The reviewed example is CAT Generation Seven with a single routing
buckle. Confirm the actual device at the school station rather than
importing another generation or improvised belt technique.

40
00:08:16,416 --> 00:08:29,736
Keep source control active while exposing and preparing. If the scene
prevents deliberate placement, follow the applicable emergency
procedure and communicate exactly what was applied and where.

41
00:08:29,736 --> 00:08:41,688
On the reviewed Gen Seven device, route the band through its single
buckle path and pull the band firmly back onto itself. Excess slack
reduces effective circumferential compression.

42
00:08:41,688 --> 00:08:50,376
Tighten the windlass to stop the bleeding, then secure it in the clip
and retain the rod and band with the time strap according to the
device instructions.

43
00:08:50,376 --> 00:09:03,528
Do not stop after a memorized number of turns. Limb size, slack and
application quality vary; the clinically relevant endpoint is control,
not the appearance of having completed the motions.

44
00:09:03,528 --> 00:09:15,720
Practice on the instructor-approved trainer and limb model. Do not
repeatedly fully occlude an uninjured classmate or reuse a clinical
single-use device as though a practice reset renewed it.

45
00:09:15,720 --> 00:09:27,912
Look at the wound after locking the device. The reviewed CAT
instructions also require a distal pulse check: continuing bleeding or
a persisting distal pulse means reassessment of effectiveness.

46
00:09:27,912 --> 00:09:39,960
Further tightening or an additional device immediately proximal and
beside the first may be needed under the applicable instruction. Do
not place a second device distal to the bleeding source.

47
00:09:39,960 --> 00:09:54,000
An effective tourniquet intentionally sacrifices distal arterial flow
to control life-threatening loss. This differs from a routine splint
circulation check and does not justify periodically letting blood
through.

48
00:09:54,000 --> 00:10:09,072
Keep it visible, record the application time and provide indicated
analgesia without releasing control. If prolonged scene or conveyance
time approaches the guideline concern, consult rather than
automatically removing it.

49
00:10:09,072 --> 00:10:21,000
The Week Four outline specifies a sixty-second CAT performance target.
Rehearse an efficient sequence on the school trainer instead of
slowing down to narrate a long essay during application.

50
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:32,424
Speed without correct position, effective compression and secure
locking is not useful. Record the time and report the observed
bleeding endpoint as distinct facts.

51
00:10:32,424 --> 00:10:45,672
The saved Week Four outline does not fully define the starting device
configuration or every clock boundary. Obtain that actual station
instruction rather than claiming an invented official pass rubric.

52
00:10:45,672 --> 00:10:59,568
On a real patient, do not wait for sixty seconds to act or stop
correcting an ineffective device because the practice clock ended.
Urgency, effective control and reassessment remain the clinical
priorities.

53
00:10:59,568 --> 00:11:12,216
For an eligible deep wound, packing transfers pressure toward the
bleeding source rather than merely absorbing blood at the skin
opening. Use the actual gauze or hemostatic product and trained
method.

54
00:11:12,216 --> 00:11:23,184
The reviewed local procedure maintains pressure during tight packing,
then applies very firm direct pressure for at least three minutes
before securement and ongoing reassessment.

55
00:11:23,184 --> 00:11:37,248
Do not import the textbook suggestion of a guaranteed two-minute
hemostatic effect. Material, wound, medication and application quality
differ, and ongoing bleeding means the endpoint has not been reached.

56
00:11:37,248 --> 00:11:52,392
Neck, chest and abdomen are excluded by the reviewed PR04. A wound
that cannot be packed safely needs its appropriate alternative
strategy and rapid definitive care, not improvised insertion into a
body cavity.

57
00:11:52,392 --> 00:12:01,080
Before the rescue lift or stretcher movement, check that dressings and
tourniquets are secure and that the team knows which injury needs
protection.

58
00:12:01,080 --> 00:12:13,152
After movement, look again for renewed bleeding and device
displacement. A higher blood pressure can also reveal an inadequate
application that seemed to work during profound shock.

59
00:12:13,152 --> 00:12:24,120
Reassess the patient beyond the wound. A continuing rapid weak pulse
or worsening breathing requires a broader explanation than the
statement that the bandage looks dry.

60
00:12:24,120 --> 00:12:35,328
Call out any recurrence, the correction and its effect. Document a
change honestly instead of rewriting the record to make control appear
uninterrupted.

61
00:12:35,328 --> 00:12:48,120
In a separate original example, a fifty-seven-year-old has pelvic pain
after a side-impact collision. The pulse is one hundred sixteen and
pressure eighty-eight over fifty-six, with cool skin.

62
00:12:48,120 --> 00:13:01,992
The mechanism and perfusion findings raise concern for significant
pelvic injury and concealed bleeding. Absence of an obvious open wound
or dramatic abdominal distension does not make the patient stable.

63
00:13:01,992 --> 00:13:14,016
Assess gently and do not repeatedly rock the pelvis to prove
instability. Search for associated injuries and prepare early
indicated binding with a coordinated movement plan.

64
00:13:14,016 --> 00:13:27,936
A binder is part of source-directed hemorrhage control and urgent
conveyance, not a definitive diagnosis. Continue airway, breathing and
circulation reassessment while arranging the appropriate trauma
destination.

65
00:13:27,936 --> 00:13:42,960
Use the actual indication rather than binding every painful hip. The
reviewed criteria combine a major mechanism with findings such as
instability of perfusion, pelvic pain, reduced consciousness or a
distracting major injury.

66
00:13:42,960 --> 00:13:52,992
Palpate the greater-trochanter level and centre the binder there. A
belt placed around the waist or over the iliac crests is too high for
the intended compression.

67
00:13:52,992 --> 00:14:06,744
An isolated neck-of-femur fracture and simple standing-height fall are
exclusions in the reviewed local procedure. An older adult with a
shortened rotated leg is not automatically an unstable pelvic-ring
case.

68
00:14:06,744 --> 00:14:19,752
Follow the real binder model for sizing, closure and tension. Do not
turn the T-POD cutting instructions or a textbook folded-sheet method
into a universal procedure for every commercial product.

69
00:14:19,752 --> 00:14:32,760
Plan application and transfer to avoid unnecessary rolling of a
suspected unstable pelvis. The movement leader coordinates the crew
and explains the plan to the patient when possible.

70
00:14:32,760 --> 00:14:44,448
After each movement, check binder position and tension and repeat the
broader patient assessment. Improvement in one pressure reading does
not exclude continued bleeding elsewhere.

71
00:14:44,448 --> 00:14:56,784
Do not routinely loosen a working binder for another stability
examination. If it becomes displaced or is accidentally released,
address the problem and communicate the actual sequence.

72
00:14:56,784 --> 00:15:09,408
Record application time, the indication, findings before and after,
and any device problem. The receiving team needs a defensible clinical
story, not just the phrase pelvis bound.

73
00:15:09,408 --> 00:15:22,752
Assess distal circulation, sensation and motor function before
stabilization when it does not delay life-saving control. Compare
relevant findings with the opposite limb and record limitations.

74
00:15:22,752 --> 00:15:33,912
Support the injury, provide indicated analgesia and choose the
appropriate splint. Do not force movement through an injured joint
merely to complete a motor checklist.

75
00:15:33,912 --> 00:15:49,176
The assigned provincial guide allows limited realignment in selected
gross deformity or distal pulselessness circumstances. That is not
unrestricted fracture reduction; use the actual taught procedure and
stop if the plan is unsafe.

76
00:15:49,176 --> 00:16:03,576
Recheck after splinting and movement. New pain, numbness, weakness or
impaired perfusion can indicate a device or injury problem requiring
prompt reassessment, not a normal price of immobilization.

77
00:16:03,576 --> 00:16:14,640
If an extremity part is separated, manage the patient first. Do not
let a search for the part postpone airway support, life-threatening
bleeding control or urgent departure.

78
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:25,032
The assigned guide describes gentle removal of gross contamination,
saline-moistened sterile gauze, a protective bag and cool protected
storage with the patient.

79
00:16:25,032 --> 00:16:36,768
Do not place tissue directly on ice, immerse it in water, use dry ice
or warm it. Protect the part from additional damage and keep its
identity linked to the correct patient.

80
00:16:36,768 --> 00:16:48,744
Communicate what was recovered and how it was handled. Replantation
suitability is determined by the receiving specialists, not guaranteed
by a particular appearance or transport promise.

81
00:16:48,744 --> 00:17:01,368
A crush injury is local tissue damage from compression. Crush syndrome
is a systemic problem that can follow substantial muscle injury and
release; not every briefly trapped finger develops it.

82
00:17:01,368 --> 00:17:13,368
Damaged muscle can release potassium and other cellular contents when
circulation returns. Fluid shifts and metabolic disturbance can worsen
perfusion and trigger dangerous dysrhythmias.

83
00:17:13,368 --> 00:17:24,840
Muscle breakdown also creates risk for later kidney injury. Dark urine
may be a clue when present, but it is not necessary before the team
prepares for a dangerous release.

84
00:17:24,840 --> 00:17:36,552
Degree of force, muscle mass, duration and associated injury influence
risk. Do not convert a single entrapment-time number into a universal
safe-release guarantee.

85
00:17:36,552 --> 00:17:49,680
Coordinate the lift with the rescue lead and the clinical team.
Request advanced resources early when indicated, and prepare for
sudden deterioration rather than treating release as the end of the
problem.

86
00:17:49,680 --> 00:18:03,672
The current crush guideline recommends consultation for the pre-
release fluid and tourniquet plan. A tourniquet for an uncontrolled
bleed and a considered pre-release crush strategy are related but
distinct indications.

87
00:18:03,672 --> 00:18:18,312
Salbutamol for the crush-related electrolyte concern requires
CliniCall consultation in that guideline. Do not give the drug just
because it is carried, or import advanced electrolyte treatment into
independent PCP practice.

88
00:18:18,312 --> 00:18:32,784
After release, repeat airway, breathing, circulation and rhythm
assessment and move toward appropriate definitive care. A normal
initial tracing does not guarantee that the subsequent course will
remain normal.

89
00:18:32,784 --> 00:18:45,264
For uncontrolled hemorrhage, clear crystalloid does not replace
oxygen-carrying red cells or definitive hemostasis. Excess fluid can
dilute coagulation and worsen the situation.

90
00:18:45,264 --> 00:18:59,712
Selected major-trauma pathways use limited crystalloid and permissive
hypotension while maintaining critical perfusion. This is a specific
clinical strategy, not permission to ignore worsening mentation or a
falling pressure.

91
00:18:59,712 --> 00:19:14,952
Suspected brain injury and clear spinal cord deficit create important
exceptions to a low-pressure approach. State the applicable pathway
and seek clinical support when priorities conflict rather than
averaging incompatible targets.

92
00:19:14,952 --> 00:19:30,168
Assess the indication, delivery and response for each actual fluid
increment. New respiratory distress, crackles or worsening perfusion
demands reassessment; a planned maximum is not a required volume to
finish.

93
00:19:30,168 --> 00:19:41,616
Tranexamic acid reduces fibrin breakdown. It does not mechanically
close a vessel, replace lost blood or remove the need for pressure, a
binder or definitive care.

94
00:19:41,616 --> 00:19:54,192
Use the actual trauma criteria, timing, exclusions and authorized
route. An impressive wound without the indicated systemic context is
not automatically the same as the guideline treatment group.

95
00:19:54,192 --> 00:20:06,120
The current service monograph and a school calculation label are
different sources. Do not convert an exercise answer into an order or
use the trauma medicine for gastrointestinal bleeding.

96
00:20:06,120 --> 00:20:19,464
When indicated, verify patient, product, dose, route, access and
delivery time, then reassess and document. Continue all essential
hemorrhage and conveyance actions in parallel.

97
00:20:19,464 --> 00:20:30,960
A bleeding patient can become cold even on a day that feels
comfortable to the crew. Exposure, wet clothing, cold ground and large
fluid volumes all matter.

98
00:20:30,960 --> 00:20:42,696
Expose to locate injury, then cover again. Insulation beneath the
patient is as important as a blanket on top; use the vehicle and
approved warming equipment appropriately.

99
00:20:42,696 --> 00:20:55,608
Preventing hypothermia helps protect coagulation, but must not hide a
recurrent bleed or obstruct airway access. Arrange warming around the
ongoing treatment and observation plan.

100
00:20:55,608 --> 00:21:07,032
A heater is not the treatment for environmental heat stroke, and burn
cooling requires simultaneous whole-patient protection. The next
lecture separates these different temperature problems.

101
00:21:07,032 --> 00:21:19,680
After release and transfer, the original worker becomes more
breathless, one side is quieter and pressure falls. The thigh
tourniquet remains secure without renewed visible bleeding.

102
00:21:19,680 --> 00:21:32,688
Do not conclude that every abnormality must be the thigh or crush
syndrome. Reassess the primary survey and consider a chest injury,
continued concealed loss and other supported explanations.

103
00:21:32,688 --> 00:21:46,512
Support oxygenation and ventilation as needed, request the appropriate
advanced response and communicate the change. Recognition of a likely
advanced procedure need does not grant independent authority to
perform it.

104
00:21:46,512 --> 00:21:56,952
State what changed, what should have improved and what got worse. Your
handover must include both the successful limb control and the new
unresolved threat.

105
00:21:56,952 --> 00:22:07,776
The school Saturday includes a five-minute secondary medication-math
checkpoint. These are original preparation questions, not the
recovered school test or its pass score.

106
00:22:07,776 --> 00:22:20,424
For a simulation-only label of one hundred milligrams per millilitre,
one point five grams is fifteen hundred milligrams. Dividing by one
hundred milligrams per millilitre gives fifteen millilitres.

107
00:22:20,424 --> 00:22:34,008
For two hundred fifty millilitres over forty minutes, the pump rate is
three hundred seventy-five millilitres per hour. With fifteen drops
per millilitre, the gravity rate is about ninety-four drops per
minute.

108
00:22:34,008 --> 00:22:44,952
Write the units and verify the answer against the label and time. No
amount, rate or drug implied by this exercise is an instruction to
treat the trauma patient.

109
00:22:44,952 --> 00:22:55,560
MIST organizes mechanism, injuries, signs and treatments. Include the
relevant event and entrapment timing, not every detail gathered from
bystanders.

110
00:22:55,560 --> 00:23:06,672
Describe the important findings and what remains suspected. Keep a
clinical concern distinct from a confirmed diagnosis and identify an
unexamined region honestly.

111
00:23:06,672 --> 00:23:21,480
Give the trend: initial pressure, later deterioration, effective limb
control, application time and the current breathing concern. The
receiver needs the direction of travel, not just a single old set of
vitals.

112
00:23:21,480 --> 00:23:33,696
Finish with what is needed now and confirm transfer of responsibility.
MIST is the school rehearsal tool; use any additional operational
handover fields required by the receiving service.

113
00:23:33,696 --> 00:23:46,536
At the first station, identify the indication and demonstrate the
actual CAT sequence on the approved model. Record performance time
separately from whether the bleeding-control endpoint was achieved.

114
00:23:46,536 --> 00:23:57,120
At the second station, explain why the pelvis should or should not be
bound, identify the greater trochanters and demonstrate the supervised
device and movement plan.

115
00:23:57,120 --> 00:24:09,288
At the third station, brief the rescue release and respond to a
changing patient. Include an immediate clinical contingency, request
for support and concise MIST handover.

116
00:24:09,288 --> 00:24:22,056
Across stations, use closed loop, protect warmth and reassess after
intervention or movement. A correct spoken answer does not by itself
establish a safe hands-on performance.

117
00:24:22,056 --> 00:24:36,744
The school links add helpful perspectives on musculoskeletal injury,
chest trauma, spinal motion, brain injury, tranexamic acid,
hypothermia and older adults. They do not all have the same review
depth or authority.

118
00:24:36,744 --> 00:24:52,248
Several articles date from 2009 to 2016 and some explain United States
practice. Keep their mechanisms and cases separate from any old dose,
threshold, device landmark or provider authorization.

119
00:24:52,248 --> 00:25:11,568
Use the current BCEHS pathway and monograph for BC practice. H06
essentials prefer a commercial vented chest seal or leaving an open
wound open, while its FR subsection still includes three-sided-
dressing wording; confirm the applicable local instruction rather than
averaging the two.

120
00:25:11,568 --> 00:25:25,584
CRASH-3 provides trial evidence for defined adults and timing. It is
not a diagnosis, a universal indication or permission to treat;
eligibility remains governed by the current local protocol.

121
00:25:25,584 --> 00:26:11,592
Complete the literal pressure definitions before applying the trauma
trend. Systolic pressure reflects the arterial peak during ventricular
ejection; diastolic pressure remains between beats and is not zero
because the ventricle relaxes. Pulse pressure is their difference: a
reading of 118 over 76 has a pulse pressure of 42 millimetres of
mercury. Stroke volume is volume per beat, not the force required for
a beat. Pulse pressure is influenced by ejection, arterial properties
and valve disease and does not directly measure stroke volume in
millilitres. Do not turn the source adult stroke-volume example into a
universal diagnostic interval or infer a blood-loss percentage from
one cuff reading.

122
00:26:11,592 --> 00:27:00,000
The actual assigned factor page names cardiac output, peripheral
resistance, circulating volume, viscosity and vessel-wall properties.
Cardiac output is heart rate times stroke volume. Resistance relates
the pressure difference to flow; compliance describes how readily a
vessel expands with added volume or pressure. They are not synonyms,
despite the source heading defining resistance as compliance. A
narrower vessel can increase resistance, greater viscosity can impede
flow, and stiff arteries alter pulsatile pressure. These influences
can change together, so an increase in one does not guarantee a fixed
final pressure. Compensatory vasoconstriction can preserve a cuff
value while hemorrhage and tissue-perfusion failure continue.

123
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:46,872
Separate atrial filling responses from the arterial pressure reflex.
In the usual arterial baroreflex, rising pressure increases stretch
signalling from the carotid-sinus and aortic regions; the response
reduces sympathetic drive and increases cardiac vagal influence,
tending to lower rate and vascular tone. A fall in pressure tends
toward the opposite response. The source paragraph about atrial
filling and a faster rate must not be memorized as rising arterial
pressure always speeds the heart. Atrial-volume reflexes, illness and
medicines add context. Reassess mentation, skin, breathing, pulse and
the treated injury rather than declaring successful perfusion from
pressure alone.

124
00:27:46,872 --> 00:28:37,680
Use the assigned tissue glossary as a structure-to-function map.
Histology studies tissue organization microscopically; it is not an
extra bedside test. The basal lamina is part of the epithelial
attachment interface, whereas the basement membrane also includes
connective support. Ground substance is the nonfibrous matrix around
cells; a fibroblast actively makes matrix and a fibrocyte is a less
active form. The tissue-healing figure shows a clot, inflammatory
cleanup, new capillaries and fibroblast-rich repair followed by a
contracting scar. Angiogenesis names new vessel growth, not infection.
Histamine contributes to dilation and permeability, while clotting
limits blood loss: these simultaneous responses do different jobs.

125
00:28:37,680 --> 00:29:28,776
For the assigned cancer pages, separate a DNA-sequence mutation from
an epigenetic change in how genetic information is used. Neither term
means every altered cell has become cancer. Changes affecting growth
control can accumulate; invasive malignant cells can cross tissue
boundaries and may later establish distant disease. The source figure
depicts disordered growth breaching the supporting layer, not proof
that every tumor has already metastasized. New vessels may support
either ordinary repair or tumor growth, depending on context. Surgery,
radiation, cytotoxic medicines and selected molecular treatments act
through different mechanisms and can affect healthy tissue too. This
reading explains biology; it does not select a treatment for a
particular patient.

126
00:29:28,776 --> 00:30:21,432
Decode the bone glossary without turning names into procedures.
Osseous tissue means bone tissue; osteogenesis and ossification
concern bone formation, with an ossification center marking a
developing formation site. An osteoid scaffold is not yet fully
mineralized bone. Articulation names a joint between skeletal
elements; a nutrient foramen is an entry for supply, and
central/Haversian versus perforating/Volkmann canals describe
different routes inside bone. A lacuna houses a bone cell; canaliculi
connect its processes to the surrounding network. An orthopedist
specializes in musculoskeletal care. These distinctions explain why
fracture can disrupt structure and supply, while open reduction still
means surgical exposure rather than an open skin wound.

127
00:30:21,432 --> 00:31:14,376
Connect the final muscle terms to adaptation rather than memorizing
similar prefixes. Hypertrophy increases the size of existing fibers;
hyperplasia means an increase in cell number, not a synonym for one
observed cell division or for ordinary skeletal training enlargement.
Atrophy loses size and functional reserve. ATPase activity breaks down
ATP, and phosphocreatine transfers phosphate to help restore ATP
rapidly; neither creates energy from nothing. Myoglobin holds oxygen
inside muscle, while angiogenesis can expand the capillary supply
during adaptation. These changes support sustained metabolism but do
not make active muscle independent of circulation. A trapped, ischemic
muscle therefore cannot be judged safe simply because it once had good
endurance.

128
00:31:14,376 --> 00:32:10,416
Read the twitch graph before using its vocabulary. A stimulus is
followed by a latent interval while excitation and calcium coupling
develop, then rising mechanical tension and finally relaxation. No
detectable tension yet does not mean no electrical or cellular
activity. The tension-time trace, the recording apparatus and a
membrane action potential are distinct things; do not use the glossary
instrument shorthand to confuse them. Recruitment and firing frequency
grade whole-muscle force. An isometric hold can generate force without
visible movement; controlled eccentric lowering generates force while
the active muscle lengthens. For an original example, compare lifting
a light bag, lowering it slowly and holding it still: shortening,
active lengthening and near-constant length do not mean on, off and
off.

129
00:32:10,416 --> 00:32:20,184
You should now be able to explain why catastrophic hemorrhage is
treated immediately in this scenario and how the crew continues airway
and breathing support in parallel.

130
00:32:20,184 --> 00:32:31,464
Choose the appropriate bleeding-control method, demonstrate correct
CAT and binder landmarks in supervised practice, and distinguish the
school timer from actual clinical success.

131
00:32:31,464 --> 00:32:43,872
Recognize that concealed bleeding, crush physiology and a new chest
problem may coexist. Reassessment must test your first explanation
rather than merely repeat the first treatment.

132
00:32:43,872 --> 00:32:54,168
Use the paired Nancy reading and school-linked trauma reading to
compare mechanisms, research claims and current local guidance, then
rehearse the original checks and MIST.
